[POL.FullName]
Poland 1939
[POL.Description]

[POL.Epilogue]
From September 17 to October 5, 1939, the pockets of Polish resistance were suppressed one by one. On September 27, Warsaw fell. The next day — Modlin. On October 1, the Baltic naval base Hel capitulated. The last center of organized Polish resistance was suppressed in Kotsk (north of Lublin), where 17,000 Poles from a Separate operational group "Polesie" under the command of General Franciszek Kleeberg surrendered on October 6.

In addition to the partisan movement within the country, numerous Polish military formations continued the war as part of the Allied armies.
Poland did not officially capitulate to Germany, even though it was completely defeated and subordinated to its troops. Until recently, the partisans fought against the forces of the fascists, even there were formations of Poles in the armies of the allies. Shortly before the defeat, an underground was organized.

All the lands of Poland were divided between the USSR and the Third Reich. The border Agreement was signed in Moscow on September 28, 1939. The lands east of the San and Bug rivers belonged to the USSR, became part of Belarus and Ukraine. In fact, the border almost exactly followed the outlines of the "Curzon line", which was recommended by the peace conference in Paris in 1919 as the eastern border of Poland. This is how it was possible to distinguish the areas in which Ukrainians, Belarusians and Poles lived.

Germany expanded the borders of East Prussia, moving them close to Warsaw, and included the area up to the city of Lodz, renamed Litzmannstadt, in the Wart region, which occupied the territories of the old Poznan region. By Hitler's decree of October 8, 1939, Poznan, Pomerania, Silesia, Lodz, part of the Kielce and Warsaw voivodeships, where about 9.5 million people lived, were proclaimed German lands and annexed to Germany. In the remaining small territory, the Nazis established the Polish General Government, whose capital was Krakow. Here the German government pursued a brutal racial policy.

[P001.FullName]
Assault into Poland
[P001.Description]
The inevitability of war became evident even to the most peaceful European politicians after the incorporation of Czechoslovakia as a protectorate of Germany in March 1939. According to a Pact signed between Germany and the USSR shortly thereafter, the so-called “Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact,” the Soviet and German governments were obligated not to attack one other. Both governments considered Western countries – France, the USA, but especially Great Britain – to be their strategic partners. But this pact, being right-minded on all accounts, also had a downside: a secret protocol, according to which the countries lying between Germany and the USSR were divided into spheres of interest of the two nations. Thus, the occupation was predetermined. The USSR intended to get Moldavia and the Baltic States, Germany wanted Poland, but leaving its eastern part up to Brest to the Russians. Right after the two giants – Stalin’s Communist USSR and Hitler’s Germany – protected themselves from each other with the Pact, the fate of the Eastern Europe, as well as the start of the Second World War was set.

Marshal Rydz-Smigly, the Commander-in-Chief of the Polish armed forces, and his staff realized that the war was imminent. According to the Rydz-Smigly’s plan, six Armies, consisting of 30 Infantry Divisions, 10 Reserve Divisions and 22 Cavalry Brigades, were deployed along the border; the reserves of these armies and the principal reserve were located in the Warsaw region. After mobilization the Polish commander had more than 50,000 officers and 1.7 million soldiers at his disposal.

The Polish campaign began on September 1, 1939 with German air strikes on Polish territory.  The considerable forces that Germany amassed out numbered the Polish army. The down side of the Polish defense was also their insufficient material status, especially in aviation. The offensive was coming from the North – Eastern Prussia, from the West and South. The Polish defense was incoherent. There were considerable gaps between the defending units. Moreover, the insufficiency of reserve also affected the situation. The defense of the southwestern part of Poland was assigned to the “Krakow” Army, which suffered from all the classic drawbacks of the Polish armed forces. Especially slogging combats in this area took place in the front sector, where on September 1st the 10th German Army was attacking with the 16th Motorized Corps. Starting at 8 am the 4th Panzer Division attacked the Volhynia Cavalry Brigade in the Mokra region. The German offensive detachment was aggressively driven back by the Uhlans Regiment. Two hours later the same Cavalry Regiment repelled the second tank attack with shellfire; only 12 tanks remained on the battlefield. Around noon the German units delivered another attack without any reconnaissance. Tanks were moving in compact columns, and came under fire from Polish batteries. The Panzer Division lost 20 light tanks and was forced to retreat.

[P002.FullName]
Rising Tide
[P002.Description]
While the western governments were thinking of whether or not they should declare war, the German army was aggressively driving the Poles deep into the center of the country by breaking through with their tanks and the extensive use of artillery and aviation. The skepticism that was natural for the German forces concerning the beginning of war was easily overcome. The Polish forces were dispersed into several large, hastily retreating groups. The battle at Bzura became the largest in central Poland, which took place to the West of Warsaw from September 9 through 18 between the Polish Poznan Army and part of the Pomorze Army forces, on one side, and formations of the German 8th and 10th Armies, on the other. The Vistula River on the North, its tributary Bzura – on the South, and the towns of Kutno and Sochaczew on West and East limited the battlefield. The Poznan Army forces, which were not yet involved in action, were moving into this region and further towards Warsaw under the threat of a German flanking attack. The fact that the army was already retreating for more than a week, and the enemy had gotten deep inside the Polish territory, built the ground for decision that was soon made by General Kutrzeba, the Commander of the Army. The idea was not just move towards Warsaw, dodging the enemy, but to strike against the German 8th Army. The plan for this onslaught was developed regardless of the unfavorable balance of power, in violation of the direct prohibition of Rydz-Smigly. 

The situation in the Bzura area contributed to the initiation of the Poznan Army offensive. It became possible thanks to a miscalculation by the German Command, which assumed that the Polish army had been completely devastated and that there was no danger of any active counter-attacks. Right after the completion of the frontier battles, the German 8th Army was assigned to chase the Poles and break into Lowicz, while the main body was deployed between Lodz and Bzura. As a result, the 8th Army was hit by a heavy flank attack by the retreating Poznan Army. The Commander's intention was of quite limited nature: with the partial Army forces to make a strike from the Kutno area towards Strykow, defeat the northern flank group of the German 8th Army and thus support the further retreat of the Poznan and Pomorze Armies to Warsaw. For the main strike General Kutrzeba built a task force under the command of General E. Knoll-Kownacki consisting of three Infantry Divisions and a heavy Artillery Regiment. On September 8th, the group deployed along a front of 24 kilometers on the northern bank of Bzura. The Knoll’s group launched the offensive during the night of September 9th, when the German aviation could not wrest it down. The unexpected onslaught pushed the Wehrmacht detachments back several kilometers to the south of Bzura.  


[P003.FullName]
A Desperate Defense
[P003.Description]
During the first two weeks of war the Poles had to cover 50-200 kilometers retreating, having left Poznan and Krakow to the enemy. Warsaw was now in direct danger of attack. Poland was desperately defending, counting upon military actions from England and France against Germany. At the same time, on the East, the USSR Army was getting ready, as it was presented by the Soviet propaganda, for a “liberation campaign, aimed at taking the population of Western Ukraine and Western Belarus under protection,” but in fact – it was for the occupation of Polish territory. 

Written directions of the Command stated that the danger of German tank breakthrough made it necessary to organize the defense of Warsaw from the South, develop the defense of bridges and at the same time take necessary measures for their destruction, if required. Two defensive grounds were established in the Central Vistula: northern — from Modlin to Deblin, and southern – further on to Sandomierz. 

The second area that the Polish Command considered to be of the utmost importance was to maintain the southern region, adjacent to the Romanian boarder, where the General Sosnkowski’s Army group was holding ground. The Command order stated: “Sosnkowski’s Army group must maintain the eastern Lesser Poland and Romanian boarder. The Piskor Army should maintain along Vistula from Sandomierz to the mouth of the Wieprz River. The direction of possible retreat was toward Tomaszow Lubelski. The retreat from the Vistula line – towards Wieprz – was under separate order. My intent is to have the Army on this line defending until the Kutrzeba’s group makes it through Radom towards Krasnik.” 

On September 15, von Bock ordered the Commander of the 4th Army to organize an offensive closer to the East with the first objective to get to the line of Volkovysk - Grodno. The 19th Motorized Corps was assigned with the objective to “move towards Wlodawa, Kowal with one Motorized and one Tank Division. One Motorized Division, reinforced by a Tank detachment, under the command of the 4th Army had the goal of reaching via Kobryn and Pruzhany, the line of Baranovichi — Slonim.” Thus, the 2nd Motorized Division moved towards Kobryn, and the 3rd Tank Division — towards Wlodawa, which occupied it on September 16th. The vanguards reached Lyuboml and detachments of the 22nd Motorized Corps also reached it from the South.

Having arrived at the Vistula River, the German 14th Army Corps of the 10th Army forces managed to fight through and establish bridgeheads at Deblin and Pulawy. The right-flanked 4th Army Corps conducted a forced crossing of the Vistula at Opole and Annopol and, having occupied Krasnik, moved further to Lublin. The 7th Army Corps occupied Sandomierz, conducted a forced crossing of the Vistula and the San and then moved eastwards.

